# The pipeline keeps two OSRM graphs, and these two variables say where each one
# lives. They must name different directories: the second is built by copying the
# first, so pointing both at one path would overwrite the untouched graph with a
# weighted copy of itself. The Makefile refuses that before anything is copied.
#
# Both must be absolute paths. Together they need about 30 GB, and preprocessing
# peaks about 5 GB above the untouched graph's own size (a transient copy of the
# 4.7 GB extract), so allow ~35 GB free where they share a filesystem.
#
# Prefer paths outside the repository so neither graph stays in the working tree.
# Where that is not possible (for example when only the project directory is
# visible to the Docker daemon), use .data/osrm/<instance> — it is git-ignored,
# and the per-instance directory is what gives the two graphs room side by side.

# The untouched graph: the road network exactly as OSM describes it, with no
# tariff in it. `setup_osrm` builds it, and every step that asks whether a route
# is drivable or which booths it crosses queries it. Served as `osrm-toll`.
OSRM_TOLL_PATH=/path/to/repo/.data/osrm/toll

# The toll-weighted graph: a copy of the untouched one with
# data/segment_weights.csv applied, so each tolled segment carries the time its
# tariff costs a driver. `apply_segment_weights` assembles it; nothing in the
# pipeline queries it. Served as `osrm-optimal`.
#
# This variable no longer names the graph preprocessing writes to. A tree set up
# before the toll weighting existed points it at the untouched graph: move that
# directory aside, set OSRM_TOLL_PATH to it, and give this one a fresh path.
OSRM_OPTIMAL_PATH=/path/to/repo/.data/osrm/optimal

# Subscription key for the Ulys placemark service, used by build_vinci_toll_booths
# to resolve OSM booth groups to toll operator gare codes.
#
# The service is the one the VINCI Autoroutes route planner calls; the key is the
# one that client presents. Read it from the network tab of a route search on
# https://www.vinci-autoroutes.com — it is the `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key` header.
# Keys rotate, so expect to refresh this occasionally.
ULYS_SUBSCRIPTION_KEY=

# The date every toll passage is priced at, used by build_vinci_prices.
#
# Tariffs change on a date. One value for the whole run is what makes
# data/vinci_price.yaml internally consistent: two rows priced weeks apart would
# disagree for a reason nothing in the file records. Change it to reprice, and
# delete data/vinci_price.yaml so the whole file is rebuilt against the new date.
#
# ISO 8601 with an explicit zone, exactly as the service expects it.
ULYS_PASSAGE_DATE=2026-08-24T12:00:00.0Z

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The application. Everything below this line is read by the `web`, `bot`, and
# `tests` services and by nothing in the pipeline. All three sit behind Compose
# profiles, so a checkout that only builds the pipeline can leave them unset.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Token for the Telegram bot, from BotFather.
#
# The `bot` service cannot start without it. That is why it is behind the `app`
# profile rather than started by a plain `docker compose up`: a pipeline-only
# checkout has no reason to hold a token, and a crash-looping bot would make
# `up` useless for the pipeline.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=

# Key for the LocationIQ autocomplete service, used by the web application's
# /api/autocomplete endpoint to turn what a user types into coordinates.
#
# Only the `web` service reads it. Routing and pricing do not, so the bot and
# the test suite work without it.
LOCATIONIQ_API_KEY=

# The public address of this service. REQUIRED: the `web` service and the `bot`
# exit at start-up, naming it, when it is unset or empty.
#
# The three directions links in every answer are built from it, so a link in a
# Telegram message comes back here to be refined before it goes on to Google. A
# service that does not know its own address cannot produce them, and answering
# with links of another shape would silently change a contract the bot and the
# page both read.
#
# It is not part of what is cached. Change it and every cached journey serves the
# new address on the next request, with no purge.
#
# The `evaluation` service reads it too, to build the links its report prints.
PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://french-tolls.undiscoveredlatitudes.com

# Host port the front end is published on, bound to 127.0.0.1.
#
# This is the one public port of the project. It is answered by the `nginx`
# service, which serves the page from www/ and proxies /api/ to the application.
# The application itself publishes nothing: the page fetches /api/ relative to
# its own origin, so the two have to answer on one address, and a second public
# port would serve the API without the page.
#
# Put a reverse proxy in front of it — nothing here terminates TLS or
# authenticates anybody.
#
# Where nothing else terminates TLS, this has to be the port PUBLIC_SITE_URL
# names. Change one and not the other and every link the service emits points at
# a port nothing listens on: the answers stay correct and every link is dead,
# which nothing reports.
FRENCH_TOLLS_PORT=8080

# Where the application finds the two routers.
#
# Defaults address them by Compose service name, which is the only path this
# project uses: the published host ports are for inspection, and on some hosts
# they refuse connections while the Compose network answers. Override only to
# point the application at routers outside this Compose project.
OSRM_TOLL_URL=http://osrm-toll:5000
OSRM_OPTIMAL_URL=http://osrm-optimal:5000

# The most intermediate waypoints the application will put in a Google Maps
# link. Nine is what a Maps directions URL carries; above it Maps drops the
# surplus without saying so.
#
# Where the optimal route needs more than this, the answer reports how many it
# needed and marks the link incomplete rather than emitting a link that lies.
# Lower it to exercise that path.
MAX_WAYPOINTS=9

# How many routing requests one journey's waypoint search may issue before it
# returns what it has verified so far and marks the answer incomplete.
#
# A request against these graphs takes 2-11 ms, so this bounds the search by
# what it actually costs. Measured: Lyon to Paris needs 2 waypoints and 99
# requests; Strasbourg to Nantes needs 8 and 325, the dearest journey found.
QUERY_BUDGET=500

# How far, in metres, an origin or a destination may be from the road network
# before the query is refused.
#
# The bot's reply text says a kilometre, so changing this makes the two
# disagree.
SNAP_REACH_M=1000

# Log level for the web application and the bot.
LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The paid evaluation. Read only by the `evaluation` service, which runs when it
# is named and never as part of `make all` or `make test`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Key for the Google Routes API, used to ask Google to drive the routes this
# project computes so that the waypoint algorithm can be measured against it.
#
# This is the only credential in the project that costs money to use. A full
# sweep of 50 cities is 2 450 requests at Google's Advanced tier — the tier any
# request carrying intermediate waypoints falls into — which is roughly 25 USD,
# paid once and stored forever under data/google_routes/.
#
# The variable is named for the Directions API because that is what it was
# issued for; the requests go to the Routes API, which the same key serves when
# that API is enabled on the Cloud project.
GOOGLE_DIRECTIONS_API_KEY=

# Where the evaluation finds this project's own API. It asks /api/calculate for
# the route and the waypoints under test, so that what is measured is what a
# user is given rather than a private copy of the algorithm.
FRENCH_TOLLS_URL=http://web:8080

# How many Google Routes API requests the web application may make in a calendar
# month, refining a journey's waypoints against what Google would actually drive.
#
# One request per journey, ever: the answer is stored, so the bot's link, the
# page's button, and a second visitor all read the same bought answer. Beyond
# the ceiling the service keeps working and stops refining.
#
# Zero or unset means never refine, which is also what happens with no API key.
GOOGLE_DIRECTIONS_MAX_REQUEST_PER_MONTH=0

